\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Remark}
\title{Divided coefficients and the complex convention}
\date{2026-11-17}

\p{Over a #{\mathbb Q}-algebra, \ref{fdip-00TH} gives
#{\divcoef{a}{j}=a^j/j!}. The finite exterior polynomial
therefore agrees with the nilpotent exponential, and
#{\Omega_V=\theta^N/N!}. Over #{\mathbb C},
\ref{fdip-00TL} and \ref{fdip-00TP} recover the
[intrinsic paired coefficient](fdip-00IS) and the
source identity in \ref{fdip-00IV}, with the same positive source
exponent and unbarred-first insertion.}

\p{Roby's divided-power algebra is defined by scalar, addition
and binomial-product relations; see
\citet{Ch. III, §§1–2, pp. 248–250}{roby1963lois}.
His p. 250 identity #{x^j=j!\divcoef{x}{j}} explains the relation
to factorial division when those factorials are invertible.
The exterior construction here proves its finite product and
substitution identities directly. A full divided-power structure
on an arbitrary algebra is additional data.}

\p{No invariant bilinear form on #{V} enters the paired
construction. Its coefficient is always a determinant.
An unpaired quadratic exterior form and its Pfaffian require
their own generator order and coefficient functional.}
